"""Append mechanism, proof and finite detector experiment; preserve negatives."""
import json,sys
from pathlib import Path
from docx import Document
from docx.oxml import OxmlElement
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];sys.path.insert(0,str(ROOT/'experiments'));import paper50 as P
p=ROOT/'paper/MEGA27-23b-50p.docx';d=Document(p)
title='Distance-five block detection for the actual differential channel'
assert not any(x.text==title for x in d.paragraphs)
j=json.loads((ROOT/'results/block_distance_code_audit.json').read_text())
P.h1(d,title)
P.para(d,'The full Fletcher pilot retained 1,653 accepted wrong payloads among 35,712 paired double substitutions. Those negatives remain unchanged. We now test a different audit-local code whose detection property follows from distance rather than an assumed checksum collision frequency. The current encoder is not replaced. The new code groups the 32 payload trits into eight field symbols, adds four parity symbols and maps the resulting 48 trits through the same seeded never-same differential encoder. Each field symbol occupies four consecutive trits. The code is systematic: payload trits are retained in their original order, so its first 32 DNA bases match the existing payload prefix for the same seed.')
P.h2(d,'A finite-field construction that can be checked without a package')
P.para(d,'Arithmetic uses GF(3)[x] modulo x^4+x+2. An integer from 0 to 80 stores four polynomial coefficients in little-endian base three. Addition is coefficientwise modulo three; multiplication is polynomial convolution followed by reduction. The script checks that the modulus has no monic linear or quadratic divisor. A reducible degree-four polynomial over a field must have a factor of degree at most two, so these checks establish irreducibility. Every nonzero element is also checked for a multiplicative inverse. This redundant test helps catch representation mistakes that a theoretical field name alone would not expose.')
P.para(d,'Choose twelve distinct field elements a_i with integer representations 0 through 11. The four parity-check rows are H[r,i]=a_i^r for r=0,1,2,3, with a_i^0=1 including the zero element. Given eight payload symbols, solve the four-by-four linear system on columns 8 through 11 to make Hc=0. Gaussian elimination uses the verified field inverses. No empirical fitting, checksum truncation or message-dependent parity matrix is used. Clean encoding and acceptance are tested under all four block seeds.')
P.h2(d,'Why field distance five is enough for two DNA substitutions')
P.para(d,'Any four columns of H have Vandermonde determinant equal to the product of pairwise coordinate differences. Since all coordinates are distinct, every factor is nonzero. Any smaller set can be extended to four columns and is therefore independent. A nonzero codeword cannot have support of at most four symbols, giving minimum field-symbol distance at least five. For an explicit upper bound, the script solves a dependence on the first five columns and obtains coefficients [7,8,6,2,1], with zeros elsewhere. All five coefficients are nonzero and the syndrome is zero. Consequently the field code has parameters [12,8,5], not merely a guessed distance. Tests check all 495 four-column determinants and the weight-five witness.')
P.para(d,'The inverse rotating-base mapping is local. Trit i depends on DNA bases i-1 and i, or the fixed seed and base zero at the start. Changing one base can alter only its own trit and the next trit. Changing at most two bases therefore affects at most four trits, including the last-base boundary where only one trit is affected. Grouping four trits into a field symbol cannot increase this count. If a changed DNA block remains valid under the never-same rule, it differs from the original codeword in at most four symbols. Because a different valid codeword needs at least five changed symbols, its syndrome cannot vanish. Invalid spacing is rejected earlier. This proves detection, not correction, for at most two strict substitutions anywhere in one complete 48-base block with its prescribed seed.')
P.para(d,'The proof concerns field-symbol distance, not an asserted DNA distance of five. Adjacent differential errors can cancel some changes or cross a symbol boundary; neither event increases the four-symbol upper bound. A changed valid DNA sequence cannot decode to exactly the same trit word because the seeded mapping is one-to-one. The guarantee requires known block boundaries, length and seed. Insertions, deletions, misalignment and wrong seeds are outside the proposition. It also does not say that three substitutions are always detected: six possible affected trits can reach the distance boundary. Extending the scope requires a separate proof and experiment.')
P.h2(d,'Exhaustive finite library including the check region')
P.para(d,'The eight payload blocks come from the same fixed 128-byte message as the prior low-byte and full Fletcher audits. For each 48-base candidate we enumerate all 48 times three single substitutions and choose(48,2) times nine double substitutions. This is 1,152 single and 81,216 double events across eight blocks, or 82,368 total. Unlike the paired Fletcher pilot, the new experiment includes the entire parity region and mixed payload/parity changes. Every alternative letter differs from the original. Invalid spacings and nonzero syndromes are reported separately. Among valid-spacing events, the implementation verifies the differential bound directly; both maximum affected-trit and maximum affected-symbol counts are four.')
P.table(d,'All-position strict substitutions for eight fixed distance-five blocks. Counts are enumerated events, not physical error probabilities.',['Outcome','Single events','Double events'],[['Invalid spacing',618,63308],['Nonzero syndrome',534,17908],['Accepted wrong',0,0],['Accepted unchanged',0,0],['Total',1152,81216]])
P.para(d,'The absence of accepted wrong payloads agrees with the distance proof, but the finite library alone would not justify a universal guarantee. The mathematical bound supplies that guarantee within its assumptions; the ledger checks the implemented mapping and detector on a reproducible sample of payloads. All-position counts cannot be compared as error rates against the earlier 32-position payload-only pilot. Different lengths create different event spaces, exposure and seams. The prior Fletcher collision rate remains a result of its own paired library, not a rate estimate for this candidate.')
P.h2(d,'Pay for the distance before making a recovery claim')
P.para(d,'Four field parity symbols require sixteen check trits, hence sixteen DNA bases. Total block length is 48 rather than the current 38 or full Fletcher candidate 44. Relative block-length increases are 26.32% and 9.09%, respectively. Under the same nominal three-copy convention, the asymptotic payload rate is (8/6)(32/48)/3=0.296296 bits per encoded nucleotide. The corresponding earlier rates are 0.374269 and 0.323232. These calculations charge all detector bases but omit finite file headers, padding, outer parity, primers and physical molecular copy counts. They describe candidate architecture, not a measured full-file rate or matched-budget frontier.')
P.para(d,'Stronger local detection does not imply better full-file recovery. A detector can turn wrong accepts into erasures while making each strand longer, raising the chance of a damaged block at a fixed per-base channel rate. Charging sixteen parity bases may leave fewer outer parity blocks or fewer molecular copies under a fixed total budget. The candidate has no implemented correction, outer recovery policy or consensus decoder in this audit. A joint experiment must encode complete files, pass clean channels, apply strict substitution noise to every eligible base, classify exact success, loud failure and silent corruption, and report total geometry and budget alongside constraint violations. None of those milestones is closed by this isolated block result.')
P.para(d,'Never-same mapping excludes within-block equal adjacent bases, but separate seeded blocks can still meet with equal letters. Field parity also changes GC distribution and local motifs. A geometry-matched comparison must measure complete-strand homopolymer and GC constraints instead of extrapolating the isolated mapping. The detector experiment does not establish viability of synthesis, storage in cells, sequencing or a cyborg-cell integration. The user-supplied verdict gate and all earlier negative results are preserved.')
P.h2(d,'Audit trail and next decision')
P.para(d,'Run python3 experiments/block_distance_code_audit.py. The JSON summary records modulus, coordinates, distance witness, geometry, explicit counts and limits. The JSONL ledger records every block, mutation order, position set, alternative letters and outcome. Its SHA256 is shown below on two lines for readability. Regression tests cover irreducibility, inverses, all four-column determinants, the distance-five witness, four-seed clean acceptance, ledger digest, event totals and observed differential bound. The production encoder remains unchanged, making it possible to compare this candidate later without silently replacing the baseline.')
P.para(d,j['ledger_sha256'][:32]+'\n'+j['ledger_sha256'][32:])
P.para(d,'This closes one narrow detector question: a systematic finite-field distance-five check detects every one- or two-substitution event within an aligned seeded block, at a stated sixteen-base parity cost. The full-file clean/strict-noise recovery question, rate-and-constraint matched frontier, wet-lab validation, manuscript-wide prose consistency and audited full-text body-page threshold all remain open. PDF page totals alone do not satisfy the body-page requirement.')
for row in d.tables[-1].rows:row._tr.get_or_add_trPr().append(OxmlElement('w:cantSplit'))
P.save(d,str(p))
